<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Spartrak Phase 4 — Global components wiring.

  ADDITIVE ONLY: this file is merged with (never replaces) Smartwave/porto's
  own Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml, per 10-THEME-ARCHITECTURE.md's
  Magento-overrides discipline ("prefer new templates/blocks in the child
  theme's own namespace over copy-and-modify of Porto or core templates").
  Porto's own default.xml is NOT touched by this file.

  Support Strip has no Porto or core equivalent (04-COMPONENT-INVENTORY.md:
  "Not a Porto feature — custom"), so it is added here as a new block inside
  the same page.wrapper container Porto already uses for porto_header,
  positioned immediately before it so it renders as a thin bar above the
  main header row.

  CONTENT IS IN CODE, NOT CMS BLOCKS (changed 2026-08-25).

  The Top Banner and Support Strip previously declared seven
  Magento\Cms\Block\Block children (spartrak_top_banner,
  spartrak_top_banner_badge, spartrak_support_hotline, _shipping_copy,
  _payment_copy, _whatsapp, _branches) to supply their text.

  NONE of those static blocks existed in the database. So on the live site the
  banner rendered nothing at all (its template bailed out on empty content)
  and the utility strip lost its entire right-hand cluster — which is why the
  header did not match Figma.

  Both templates now use plain __() strings, per the explicit project
  instruction that this content is handled in code. The copy remains
  changeable without a deploy because __() strings are translatable per store
  view (Arabic wording lives in this theme's i18n/ar_EG.csv), and this removes
  seven block instantiations plus seven static-block lookups from every single
  page render.
-->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <html>
        <!-- Explicit for robustness/symmetry with the RTL theme's own dir="rtl" — see that theme's Magento_Theme/layout/default.xml for the full finding. Browsers default to ltr anyway, but this documents the decision instead of relying on an implicit default. -->
        <attribute name="dir" value="ltr"/>
    </html>
    <head>
        <!--
          Mageplaza_Search frontend teardown (performance).

          MEASURED: Mageplaza_Search::search.phtml emitted
          <script src="/media/mageplaza/search/ar_0.js"> on EVERY page — a
          3,524,661-byte client-side dump of the product/category index. That
          single file was 98% of the storefront's entire JS payload
          (3,577,639 bytes total; Magento's own merged bundle is 52,863).

          IT IS NOT USED. This theme re-declares `top.search` in its own
          Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml as a plain Template on
          Magento_Search::form.mini.phtml, so the header search box that
          actually renders is Magento core's, driven by core's `quickSearch`
          widget against /search/ajax/suggest/ — verified in the rendered
          HTML. Mageplaza's instant-search dropdown never appears; only its
          index payload was being downloaded.

          SAFE TO UNHOOK: Mageplaza_Search ships no frontend controllers
          (Controller/ contains only Adminhtml) and registers no DI plugins
          or observers, so it contributes nothing to /catalogsearch/result
          or to the search backend. Removing these four frontend nodes
          removes the payload and leaves search working on Magento core.

          The module stays INSTALLED and ENABLED (mpsearch/general/enabled=1)
          — this is a theme-level teardown, not a module change, so admin
          config and the generated index file are untouched and re-enabling
          is a one-line revert.

          NOT TOUCHED: Mageplaza_LayeredNavigation and Mageplaza_AjaxLayer,
          which do render the live PLP filter sidebar (#layered-filter-block).
        -->
        <remove src="Mageplaza_Search::css/algoliasearch.css"/>
    </head>
    <body>
        <!--
          Page messages as self-dismissing toasts, PROJECT-WIDE.

          Started from a template rather than a body attribute: this theme
          already sets data-mage-init on <body> from the category and search
          handles, and <attribute> overwrites rather than merges, so a body
          binding here was silently dropped on exactly those pages. See
          templates/html/toast-init.phtml for the full note.

          The widget only observes the message container and schedules
          dismissal; it never creates, filters or suppresses a message, so both
          of Magento's message pipelines (server-rendered and the Knockout/AJAX
          one) keep working exactly as they did.
        -->
        <referenceContainer name="before.body.end">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template"
                   name="spartrak.toast.init"
                   template="Magento_Theme::html/toast-init.phtml"/>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!-- NOTE: <remove name="..."/> is head-asset syntax only; body blocks
             must be removed with remove="true" on a reference. -->
        <referenceBlock name="mageplaza.top.search" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="mp.instant.hit" remove="true"/>
        <referenceBlock name="mpsearch.algolia.configuration" remove="true"/>
        <!--
          Top Promo Banner (node 595:14466), sits above the Support Strip in
          the same page.wrapper container. Its copy, badge, icon and dismiss
          control are all owned by the template — see the file header above on
          why this no longer has CMS-block children.
        -->
        <referenceContainer name="page.wrapper">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template" name="spartrak.top_banner" template="Magento_Theme::html/top-banner.phtml" before="spartrak.support_strip"/>
        </referenceContainer>

        <referenceContainer name="page.wrapper">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template" name="spartrak.support_strip" template="Magento_Theme::html/support-strip.phtml" before="porto_header"/>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!--
          Mobile Hamburger Drawer (CONFIRMED component, 16-ARCHITECTURE-DECISIONS.md
          2026-08-23 pass: 3 states, 4 top-level accordion groups). Declared as its
          own block rather than folded into header.phtml so it can be lazily
          rendered/hydrated independently of the header on the performance side
          later (13-PERFORMANCE-ARCHITECTURE.md) without touching the header template.
          Re-uses the SAME native catalog.topnav Topmenu instance's category data
          indirectly by reading the store's category tree itself — see
          mobile-nav-drawer.phtml for the ASSUMPTION notes on its exact grouping.
        -->
        <referenceContainer name="page.wrapper">
            <block class="Magento\Theme\Block\Html\Topmenu" name="spartrak.mobile_nav_drawer" template="Magento_Theme::html/mobile-nav-drawer.phtml" ttl="3600" after="porto_header">
                <!--
                  Same admin-managed brand source the desktop mega panel uses,
                  so the mobile "البراندات" row lists real brands instead of an
                  empty-state link. One ViewModel, two consumers.
                -->
                <arguments>
                    <argument name="brand_navigation" xsi:type="object">Spartrak\Catalog\ViewModel\BrandNavigation</argument>
                    <argument name="account_menu" xsi:type="object">Spartrak\CustomerAccount\ViewModel\AccountMenu</argument>
                </arguments>
            </block>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!--
          NOTE: the Primary Mega Navigation (spartrak.primary_nav) and the
          footer link columns both live in this theme's
          Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml, NOT here — see that file's
          comment for why (module-sequence ordering: a reference's target
          must already exist, and both porto_header and footer_block are
          declared in Porto's own Smartwave_Porto bucket, which processes
          AFTER this Magento_Theme bucket. A first attempt placed both here;
          confirmed broken via a live bootstrap-script layout dump).
        -->

        <!--
          QA FIX (2026-08-23, live browser pass): the Login/Register Modal was
          originally built as a template override of Porto's own
          header_customer_form_login block (Smartwave\Porto\Block\Form\Login),
          which is gated by ifconfig="porto_settings/header/login_popup". Live
          testing confirmed that config is off by default, so the block never
          instantiates and the modal never renders — regardless of which
          template backs it. Rather than depend on an unrelated Porto admin
          toggle this project doesn't control, the modal is now its own
          independent block, unconditional. The old Porto-block-based override
          at Smartwave_Porto/templates/html/login.phtml is superseded by this
          and has been removed (dead file — Porto's own header_customer_form_login
          stays off/unused, untouched, per rule 12).
        -->
        <!--
          Backend wiring (2026-08-24): the modal was a step-switching shell whose
          forms posted to "#spartrak-auth-pending-module-1" — the placeholder
          left when Module 1 did not yet exist. Spartrak_CustomerAuth now ships
          the whole phone+OTP backend, so the modal is connected to it.

          The auth_config ViewModel supplies the OTP box count, resend cooldown
          and code lifetime from that module's store configuration, so the
          template renders whatever admin has set instead of hardcoding the 4
          boxes the Figma component draws (shipped default is 6 — see the module
          README's note that code_length and max_verify_attempts must be chosen
          as a pair).

          Deliberately a ViewModel argument rather than a custom block class:
          the block stays a plain Template with no theme-side PHP, which is the
          seam 10-THEME-ARCHITECTURE.md asks for between theme presentation and
          module business logic.

          The block remains cacheable. Everything the ViewModel exposes is
          store-scoped configuration, and full-page cache is already keyed by
          store; nothing customer-specific is rendered here. The form key is
          NOT rendered into this markup for that reason — a cached form key is a
          stale form key. The widget reads it from the form_key cookie instead
          and rewrites that cookie from each auth response, because signing in,
          registering and resetting all regenerate the session id.
        -->
        <referenceContainer name="page.wrapper">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template" name="spartrak.auth_modal" template="Magento_Theme::html/login-modal.phtml" after="spartrak.mobile_nav_drawer">
                <arguments>
                    <argument name="auth_config" xsi:type="object">Spartrak\CustomerAuth\ViewModel\AuthConfig</argument>
                    <argument name="post_login_destinations" xsi:type="object">Spartrak\CustomerAuth\ViewModel\PostLoginDestinations</argument>
                </arguments>
            </block>
        </referenceContainer>

        <!--
          ===================================================================
          THE BREADCRUMB MOVES INSIDE THE PAGE'S CONTENT BOX
          ===================================================================
          Figma 488:18402 pads the breadcrumb row by the page gutter, i.e. the
          crumb starts on the same vertical line as the content below it.

          Core puts the block in `page.top`, a child of `page.wrapper` that
          sits OUTSIDE `main.content` - so the band spans the viewport and has
          no relationship to the content column at all. On the live store the
          crumb sat hard against the window edge on the PDP while the product
          started ~190px in.

          The obvious fix is to mirror the gutter in CSS. It does not hold:
          this theme re-cuts `.page-main` PER PAGE and every page picks a
          different box -

            PLP / search   full width, 48 / 20 / 15  (components/_plp.less)
            PDP            max 1440 + 48, 16 small   (components/_pdp.less)
            cart           48, 16 small              (components/_cart.less)
            account        48, max-inline-size none  (components/_account.less)
            everything else Magento's own 1140 centred + 10

          Mirroring that is five rules that must be kept in step by hand, and
          the sixth page someone re-cuts silently breaks the crumb again.

          So the block is MOVED into `columns.top` instead, which is declared
          in Magento's own page_layout/empty.xml - the layout every other page
          layout extends via `<update handle="empty"/>`, so it exists on every
          page - and which sits INSIDE `main.content`. The breadcrumb now
          inherits whatever box that page gave `.page-main`, automatically,
          including for pages not written yet. `columns.top` declares no
          htmlTag, so it emits no wrapper: the `.breadcrumbs` div becomes a
          direct child of `.page-main` and nothing is added to the DOM.

          `before="-"` puts it first in that container, above `page.main.title`
          and the message area - which is the order Figma draws.

          The block, its class and its template are untouched; core still
          builds the crumbs and Magento_Catalog still adds the category path.
          This only changes which container renders it.
        -->
        <move element="breadcrumbs" destination="columns.top" before="-"/>

    </body>
</page>
